{
  "id": "T18_50_a_requirement_begun_by_the_review_of_one_story_has_its_records_on_that_story",
  "family": "T18",
  "rules": [
    "7.6.7",
    "7.6.15",
    "7.6.9",
    "7.6.10",
    "9.5.7",
    "9.3.1",
    "1.5",
    "12.19"
  ],
  "description": "Three change stories each record A-601's revision of a door's finish. A directive on chg-1 alone establishes a lacquer requirement for Door 101: its begin act is an Assertion on chg-1's Item, named on chg-1, and no act Item is created. A directive on chg-2 and chg-3 together establishes one for Door 102, which keeps an act Item of its own and is nested under both stories; one set directly by minutes for Door 103 keeps its own acts and has no parent. What remains lists the three requirements with their parents, and chg-1's own story read lists only its detection, its review and its own periods. Door 101's requirement is then withdrawn and established again by minutes: the end act of the first period and the begin act of the second are still recorded on chg-1, though the second period has no parent. Its fulfillment, and the revision of that end act when earlier fulfilling evidence is backfilled, are recorded on chg-1 as well.",
  "note": "7.6.7: \"An expectation property whose first period is nested under exactly one story (7.6.15), because a review of that story began it, is part of that story, its middle: its begin and end acts, and the revisions of them (7.6.10), are Assertions on that story Item in the same form ... Any other expectation property, such as one established directly from minutes (1.4), or one whose first period was begun by a review that targets several stories and so is nested under each, keeps acts of its own ... every later act of the property, in any period, is recorded in the same place ... where an act is recorded never changes a result\". README section 7: story-held acts are `(<story>, \"<story>/exp:<path>/b<n>\", <story>)`, with `/e<n>` and `/v<n>`, and \"Story-held acts create only their context Items\".",
